KW  - access
KW  - cardiovascular diseases
KW  - contribution analysis
KW  - health policy
KW  - pharmaceuticals
AB  - Background: Access to medicines remains a persistent global challenge. In Chile, several policies have sought to strengthen pharmaceutical coverage within the health system. Among them, the Explicit Health Guarantees (GES) and the Pharmacy Fund (FOFAR) represent major policy milestones. Objective: This study assesses the impact of GES and FOFAR on access to medicines for diabetes, dyslipidemia, and hypertension among beneficiaries of both public and private subsystems. Design: A contribution analysis approach was applied using a mixed-methods design. Methods: Quantitative analyses combined descriptive and quasi-experimental difference-in-differences methods based on repeated cross-sectional data from national health surveys (2003, 2009/10, 2016/17), focusing on medicine use and effective coverage. The qualitative component included ten semi-structured interviews with key informants involved in policy design and implementation. Results: Results show that both GES and FOFAR substantially contributed to improving access to medicines for hypertension, diabetes, and dyslipidemia. Quantitative findings revealed marked increases in medicine use and effective coverage, particularly between 2010 and 2017, while qualitative evidence highlighted their complementary and reinforcing policy roles. Conclusions: Taken together, our findings suggest that GES and FOFAR were likely important contributors to improvements in contact coverage and effective coverage for these chronic conditions in Chile, although these changes occurred alongside ongoing health-system strengthening efforts and should therefore be interpreted accordingly. Overall, incorporating medicines into social protection schemes-especially through enforceable guarantees-plays a key role in expanding access. Sustained impact requires adequate resources, robust logistics, and strong implementation capacity for chronic disease management.
AB  - Access to essential medicines is a challenge around the world, and many people still struggle to get the treatments they need for chronic diseases. In Chile, two major public policies­the Explicit Health Guarantees (GES) and the Primary Health Care Pharmacy Fund (FOFAR)­were created to improve access to medicines for conditions such as diabetes, high blood pressure, and high cholesterol. These conditions are common in the country and can lead to serious complications if not properly treated. This study looked at whether GES and FOFAR helped people obtain and use the medicines required to manage these chronic diseases. To do this, two types of information were combined: data from the National Health Survey (collected in 2003, 2010 and 2017), and interviews with people involved in designing and implementing these policies. The results show that both GES and FOFAR contributed to better access to medicines. Between 2010 and 2017, more people with diabetes and high blood pressure were taking their medications, and more had their conditions under control. For people with high cholesterol who receive care in the public system (FONASA), the FOFAR program also helped increase medicine use compared with those in the private system (ISAPRE), who did not receive the same benefit. Interviews with key decision-makers helped explain why these improvements occurred. They highlighted that GES established enforceable rights to receive care, while FOFAR strengthened primary care by providing extra resources specifically for medicines. Interviewees also described remaining challenges, such as logistical problems, limited resources, and persistent beliefs that free medicines are of lower quality. Overall, the study shows that integrating medicines into social protection policies can significantly improve access, but sustained progress requires good financing, strong primary care, efficient delivery systems, and regular monitoring of policy performance.
UR  - https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13494291
UR  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1177/27550834261480357
